#uuid "afea3ce6-6a9f-582e-b5b3-d9aef7133b3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452393} :answer-field/id #uuid "71913576-27b7-510d-b8b5-58432fc3e668" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d0792af-c54b-5167-a6cf-da7598a4b23d" :question/math-academy-id "q-97306" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨a,-ia⟩=-i⟨a,a⟩$ for any nonzero vector  $a\\in {C}^{n}$\n- **II.** $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- **III.** $⟨a,b⟩\\in R$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false.  By the antilinearity of $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$  in the second argument we have \n\n$$\n⟨a,-ia⟩=\\overset{―}{(-i)}⟨a,a⟩=i⟨a,a⟩\\ne -i⟨a,a⟩\n$$\n\n \nfor any nonzero vector  $a\\in {C}^{n}.$\n\n- Statement II  is true. Indeed, the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\n- Statement III is false. The inner product over $C$ is a complex number in general.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050331 :topic/id #uuid "4076cd19-9f1e-54c7-b08d-83c706b3cbae" :topic/math-academy-id 2100 :topic/title "Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b21fe66-8492-5d17-97c0-fee28d852f5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452395} {:answer/id #uuid "b3cc7c45-679e-5c92-85eb-fdc51e826da1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3cc7c45-679e-5c92-85eb-fdc51e826da1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452396} :answer-field/id #uuid "51873899-664d-5819-a145-3f3fecc5c83f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453045} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dca37905-a856-50d2-91e4-25768462720f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452397} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6a9579-4874-5317-ad50-5bd45835eb41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dca37905-a856-50d2-91e4-25768462720f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452397} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cf568a4-f601-5390-b9a6-5df7696a20d1" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453046} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f174f582-c296-57fb-90e9-5cc880809de9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452399} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c75e7a-9c06-5da5-a06b-4e1815e7d645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0c75e7a-9c06-5da5-a06b-4e1815e7d645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452400} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1e08c26-c3df-5f51-a440-819a7bbba1f5" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1d17c886-492c-5ed0-9034-9494d7fd5daf" :question/math-academy-id "q-97443" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-1,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-1,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=t$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- **II.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=\\pi $\n- **III.** $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-1}^{1}-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){|}_{-1}^{1}-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)-(-1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-1))-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-1))+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-1)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors must be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360813 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050331 :topic/id #uuid "4076cd19-9f1e-54c7-b08d-83c706b3cbae" :topic/math-academy-id 2100 :topic/title "Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2d909dc-b9cc-58f6-bb2b-25a8f80e1941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452401} {:answer/id #uuid "2392b706-0679-5870-b8f1-88b55f8a640d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2392b706-0679-5870-b8f1-88b55f8a640d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452402} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0f4ea39-f39e-545b-94b4-162cea75b15f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453048} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aba97a89-96b2-5568-a6b4-321f97061c62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452403} {:answer/id #uuid "782bd1a7-a489-5094-8b91-606fca371e0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aba97a89-96b2-5568-a6b4-321f97061c62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452403} :answer-field/id #uuid "58be794a-9c14-5e0c-9b19-1744a4067766" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453049} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3c414cf-054f-5450-8d89-dbd581b1d6f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452405} {:answer/id #uuid "82d29fd7-af00-51ac-979f-dc8f9f13b5d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3c414cf-054f-5450-8d89-dbd581b1d6f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452405} :answer-field/id #uuid "f35cb758-1173-57e7-b791-5963fbd2496d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "65fcd4f1-eb38-5308-baf7-7bd3106cdba9" :question/math-academy-id "q-97445" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,3]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,3],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=t$ and $g(t)={e}^{t},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2{e}^{3}+1$\n- **II.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=1$\n- **III.** $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{3}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{t} & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{0}^{3}-{\\int }_{0}^{3}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t{e}^{t}){|}_{0}^{3}-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(3{e}^{3}-0{e}^{0})-{e}^{t}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =3{e}^{3}-({e}^{3}-1) \\\\  & =2{e}^{3}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360814 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050331 :topic/id #uuid "4076cd19-9f1e-54c7-b08d-83c706b3cbae" :topic/math-academy-id 2100 :topic/title "Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e3a6567-add1-5ac2-9492-9b3911c4e0c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452407} {:answer/id #uuid "24cdd274-bb6d-5ed3-a90e-ef4b381ae89b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24cdd274-bb6d-5ed3-a90e-ef4b381ae89b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452408} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2602bbf-6f5c-5bef-8d30-29b248c29764" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453051} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb2c72fe-ad32-5ccd-9202-558deff20196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452409} {:answer/id #uuid "3d45eeb2-34bd-5f17-9b64-a588aa2442ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb2c72fe-ad32-5ccd-9202-558deff20196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452409} :answer-field/id #uuid "8acac60b-2493-53f4-844a-9f82af58d6b9" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453052} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb802222-f878-52f8-b931-8e6aa95957fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452411} {:answer/id #uuid "b7260ef6-a77d-5a66-9e00-8c5dabe3c051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb802222-f878-52f8-b931-8e6aa95957fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452411} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb59a9c8-6c71-559c-8e74-fd29b03c4f5f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c591f6d1-f8d9-5efb-9959-52acee26e120" :question/math-academy-id "q-97448" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-1,0]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-1,0],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{0}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=t$ and $g(t)=\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=-\\frac{1}{3}$\n- **II.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- **III.** $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{0}t\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=1-{t}^{2},$ $\\text{d}u=-2t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}t\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3/2}]-[-\\frac{1}{3}(0)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050331 :topic/id #uuid "4076cd19-9f1e-54c7-b08d-83c706b3cbae" :topic/math-academy-id 2100 :topic/title "Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1032df2a-f8af-50fb-bb60-32e1fbb1af15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452413} {:answer/id #uuid "5b6a2f02-78bb-5193-a858-1aa5b606252c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1032df2a-f8af-50fb-bb60-32e1fbb1af15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452413} :answer-field/id #uuid "48bc6f7b-8a82-5d82-9f4e-70afc162dbdc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453054} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b71135b1-b085-5c32-8a23-43a663474b20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452415} {:answer/id #uuid "02b6bc90-c2db-5bf9-aaaf-b53e23eaf104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02b6bc90-c2db-5bf9-aaaf-b53e23eaf104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452416} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f4b4ed3-1b90-5d47-b391-ee5dbbf141d1" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453055} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be11b02a-310f-5aa3-909c-b8c58993622c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452417} {:answer/id #uuid "284abb42-c669-5d39-8de9-145b358632f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "284abb42-c669-5d39-8de9-145b358632f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452418} :answer-field/id #uuid "0691d9bf-8dc8-5016-8c72-f67a4b6d362c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f2adb07-0219-5290-bbb5-6e66128c62bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-97449" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ and $g(t)=1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=\\pi $\n- **II.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- **III.** $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={[-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}{2}|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors must be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050331 :topic/id #uuid "4076cd19-9f1e-54c7-b08d-83c706b3cbae" :topic/math-academy-id 2100 :topic/title "Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ba28d4d-62f6-5ab0-9502-85dc2bfb9cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452419} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd26d9b-af6b-523c-89af-5533f38401fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ba28d4d-62f6-5ab0-9502-85dc2bfb9cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452419} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6a55637-e67c-58dd-9db5-70f22596e9c3" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453057} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23502feb-a170-544a-a725-bbccb2a2f2c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452421} {:answer/id #uuid "4aa6cce0-fa16-5915-849c-426fb302a206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23502feb-a170-544a-a725-bbccb2a2f2c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452421} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3dd7665-1aaa-5df3-b589-7b04d06e2b67" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453058} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a99d62e6-4e7d-5f91-8bf3-eb77fad6e263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452423} {:answer/id #uuid "2d925e50-7850-5f07-8db6-ba284e4a2bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d925e50-7850-5f07-8db6-ba284e4a2bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452424} :answer-field/id #uuid "46142dab-d3b3-5273-a62f-da3826c54e1a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e5f9b456-0ac1-5b4f-9e54-6e0e7b4c5409" :question/math-academy-id "q-97450" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,2\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=\\pi $\n- **II.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2\\pi $\n- **III.** $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )).$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the given identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t-2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+2t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)}{6}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12\\pi )}{6}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{6}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statements I and II are false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors must be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050331 :topic/id #uuid "4076cd19-9f1e-54c7-b08d-83c706b3cbae" :topic/math-academy-id 2100 :topic/title "Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9639d9f6-5fc6-5649-9d79-dfb677c58547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452425} {:answer/id #uuid "59dd406c-6dae-56bd-8629-711d79613c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9639d9f6-5fc6-5649-9d79-dfb677c58547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452425} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a249449-db53-5ccb-94a6-3060faa5b0a5" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453060} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50d71c0a-e5b5-5371-ba03-225fa31b5188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452427} {:answer/id #uuid "6a963d19-f4cf-50ee-aaec-c77a41bf04d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a963d19-f4cf-50ee-aaec-c77a41bf04d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452428} :answer-field/id #uuid "8161f941-02df-588e-9f7e-7a593219a5c1" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453061} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f892e29-b44f-5f63-bba5-28f968957e17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452429} {:answer/id #uuid "39c03eb7-b08f-5cc6-a0a7-a164b982ff15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f892e29-b44f-5f63-bba5-28f968957e17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452429} :answer-field/id #uuid "52103090-e08b-575c-8624-dcdfeb5d80e9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453062}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a7e7515b-c5f0-52cd-b9cf-1d4546466e79" :question/math-academy-id "q-97452" :question/problem "Let $V=C[1,{e}^{2}]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[1,{e}^{2}],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ and $g(t)=\\frac{1}{t},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- **II.** $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2$\n- **III.** $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172039 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd092f29-08bf-5425-88a4-8cba4f0a31c8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048489 :topic/id #uuid "a42b014b-839c-50bc-ab24-36abe7ee39ce" :topic/math-academy-id 3352 :topic/title "Slope Fields for Nonautonomous Differential Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95bf5c87-5d3a-5cc2-9253-891ef7730177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438628} {:answer/id #uuid "3d5d5215-469a-5799-ba24-eb8bc4c358d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95bf5c87-5d3a-5cc2-9253-891ef7730177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438628} :answer-field/id #uuid "f545d324-f9c3-507e-983b-fd1001340ff1" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439343} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33b8f3b3-189f-5b75-9ccc-ddd834e75d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438630} {:answer/id #uuid "d83106e2-c95a-541f-8d33-b2ba054c50ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d83106e2-c95a-541f-8d33-b2ba054c50ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438631} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7dbe69a-f051-5886-8cde-e2c1753c8657" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439344} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e25dae65-1d55-511a-b24e-a8cb350771d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438632} {:answer/id #uuid "41b3f2e3-0dfd-5365-982e-fd4103306c05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e25dae65-1d55-511a-b24e-a8cb350771d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438632} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8887387-6555-5bd2-8c67-a09c271d9b0f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ebbc31b-8f74-56d3-8d4f-392a8aeeda85" :question/math-academy-id "q-97477" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-xy$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive\n- **II.** At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive\n- **III.** At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative" :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y)=-xy.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/2c/2c218c7df2bf4e159b4bffaccb4296beab8e3f44ab82d56b3589050af92ccf8e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the first quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the first quadrant.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the second quadrant.\n\n- Statement III is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the fourth quadrant."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360822 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172039 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd092f29-08bf-5425-88a4-8cba4f0a31c8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048489 :topic/id #uuid "a42b014b-839c-50bc-ab24-36abe7ee39ce" :topic/math-academy-id 3352 :topic/title "Slope Fields for Nonautonomous Differential Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af5c066d-bd1b-5916-a240-8a09350060cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438634} {:answer/id #uuid "e033cbd9-4a9c-558a-b84e-515ba29c5965" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af5c066d-bd1b-5916-a240-8a09350060cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438634} :answer-field/id #uuid "86f3af7d-b3a9-5b53-9913-e3d5d3053834" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 1759218