/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aa956fd-de7f-5971-bd20-88593e558254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438645} :answer-field/id #uuid "572d6ef2-b778-5091-9ae8-150ff55b2bb6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "90e895ee-1be8-55c1-ab2d-a3596627f1b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-97487" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4x{y}^{2}$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n- **II.** At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive\n- **III.** At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative" :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y)=-4x{y}^{2}.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/96/96c937cf10d45d8870a4a56e257d184dcf2e4caf11312c05df9f385f9c5ee7a8.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the first quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the first quadrant.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the second quadrant.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the fourth quadrant."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172041 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75710e99-e13c-54ba-9401-486bfec15284" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rectangular Regions Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048489 :topic/id #uuid "a42b014b-839c-50bc-ab24-36abe7ee39ce" :topic/math-academy-id 3352 :topic/title "Slope Fields for Nonautonomous Differential Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b88eec4-4bca-5a0c-8880-b03758a9562c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438646} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b8d18f-d196-594c-8c33-0a3b063ff4fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0b8d18f-d196-594c-8c33-0a3b063ff4fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438647} :answer-field/id #uuid "a57f0197-ee41-5615-824b-b099007a4ea7" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439352} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09a982b0-26c1-598f-bb2a-081c92298f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438648} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e21d2c-a5eb-5d88-b6f1-1f1bb6ae21fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09a982b0-26c1-598f-bb2a-081c92298f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438648} :answer-field/id #uuid "30a269a3-6717-5dbd-aa51-fbb9a794d6c2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439353} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccb719f3-4ad9-549b-8f43-db9fde533258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438650} {:answer/id #uuid "b9393b52-2c4f-58b9-9b1c-b3c269d74cac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9393b52-2c4f-58b9-9b1c-b3c269d74cac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438651} :answer-field/id #uuid "e284995b-eb87-5cca-a13d-12a99f42075a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a885d23a-7465-5389-b01f-26298ac26e61" :question/math-academy-id "q-97495" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(x+2)(y+2)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>-2$ and $y>-2,$ the slope of the slope field is positive\n- **II.** At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<-2$ and $y>-2,$ the slope of the slope field is positive\n- **III.** At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<-2$ and $y<-2,$ the slope of the slope field is positive" :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y)=(x+2)(y+2).$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=-2$ or $y=-2.$ This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/7d/7d225d82eee3043380eda76506b28e81aee06d42a232348b91b0d5ceb04f3318.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/bd/bd9eedd3b8d82415e8f3126236f2e3a3b4e326a7072057bbd21406da92a08139.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. If $x>-2$ and $y>-2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $x<-2$ and $y>-2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $x<-2$ and $y<-2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362211 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172041 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75710e99-e13c-54ba-9401-486bfec15284" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rectangular Regions Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048489 :topic/id #uuid "a42b014b-839c-50bc-ab24-36abe7ee39ce" :topic/math-academy-id 3352 :topic/title "Slope Fields for Nonautonomous Differential Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4842db83-277f-5ac6-9756-7d71ed57a360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438652} {:answer/id #uuid "aff73e0e-7657-51b7-a7f5-1be162f86243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4842db83-277f-5ac6-9756-7d71ed57a360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438652} :answer-field/id #uuid "3021006b-6d7f-5efe-b7a1-726f767d922d" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439355} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a32b2ab2-8963-54b9-9a32-d18a9a92c5fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438654} {:answer/id #uuid "9b16c32e-6930-5573-ab6e-d3adf9fffe07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a32b2ab2-8963-54b9-9a32-d18a9a92c5fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438654} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9933f56-9026-5d7c-b64d-f7f6c2a23d9e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439356} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6c52061-cde6-58fd-9539-45c706276407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438656} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf6408d-db98-52dd-9aa1-9d5f8b43eba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcf6408d-db98-52dd-9aa1-9d5f8b43eba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438657} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab818855-7afa-5726-ad99-5b0158d162ae" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3ca0f65d-7276-53ff-adf8-200ee3f85dcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-97534" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(x-1)(y-1)^{2}$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<1$ and $y<1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive\n- **II.** At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>1$ and $y<1,$ the slope of the slope field is negative\n- **III.** At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>1$ and $y>1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive" :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y)=(x-1)(y-1)^{2}.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=1$ or $y=1.$ This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/07/075264fa042adc64e160a83f03a2b125dd5a9b0927fc3ed318778c0004689ec4.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/57/5716d59a745fc148b504a4db523286d7c7209fe793fcf369a90d220125f7e9fb.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. If $x<1$ and $y<1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $x>1$ and $y<1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $x>1$ and $y>1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124528 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050470 :topic/id #uuid "4c435cda-6bf3-50f8-af96-25002a1726eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1966 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "436bf24b-bdf6-5bd1-bf14-e293249ddd46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452431} {:answer/id #uuid "363b7103-4c5b-566a-8bb6-bb6db139809a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "363b7103-4c5b-566a-8bb6-bb6db139809a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452432} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed94cd18-02ea-5163-8871-843e8d20bacd" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453063} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddb395a3-0026-5fe2-8672-0d8a776661b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452433} {:answer/id #uuid "ad49d154-32c7-51a1-9c40-23951e669167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddb395a3-0026-5fe2-8672-0d8a776661b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452433} :answer-field/id #uuid "85f07249-c02b-5278-9c34-c03c810c79e2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453064} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b316a2be-90ba-5f03-af54-19e6e2c49f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452435} {:answer/id #uuid "4beb83e8-81ac-5a00-996b-7891230847a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4beb83e8-81ac-5a00-996b-7891230847a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452436} :answer-field/id #uuid "75b5380c-d718-5a65-a9f5-9759ac621a6b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a9eef89-f664-5ecc-a524-f84c3e33cc1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-97871" :question/problem "A matrix $B$ has the characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{4}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\lambda =1$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n- **II.** $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $B$\n- **III.** $B$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =1$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{4}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (1)^{4}-2(1)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ 1-2+1 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since substituting $\\lambda =2$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{4}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (2)^{4}-2(2)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ 16-8+1 & =0 \\\\ 9 & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $B.$ As a result, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $B$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124605 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050470 :topic/id #uuid "4c435cda-6bf3-50f8-af96-25002a1726eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1966 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25a42069-f918-5e71-9dd9-2a211c78e924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452437} {:answer/id #uuid "96dc8307-77bd-54e5-ae5b-b3521133ccfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25a42069-f918-5e71-9dd9-2a211c78e924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452437} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b11ffe1-abc8-57b0-8f5c-136583ab3529" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453066} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bd9b746-af0b-579f-9539-7f4c89d3f3f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452439} {:answer/id #uuid "a8139ae7-a53d-5b72-bf22-02323cba44f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8139ae7-a53d-5b72-bf22-02323cba44f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452440} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab27423b-0ac8-5d39-97b6-dba386cf4d90" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453067} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab3cdd13-53bd-57d7-9494-64f83f7502ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452441} {:answer/id #uuid "e535c4d0-7baa-5a48-acbd-a5afaa7c103d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab3cdd13-53bd-57d7-9494-64f83f7502ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452441} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a2becec-7b1e-57b8-92f8-0a9e0aaf26ff" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ed9c37d8-c5de-5898-be3f-6a097e562da9" :question/math-academy-id "q-97897" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $Bx=0$ has only zero solution\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(B)\\ne {R}^{2}$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(B)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=B-0I=B.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(B-0I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, the matrix $M=B$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $M=B$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(B)\\ne {R}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $M=B$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124605 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050470 :topic/id #uuid "4c435cda-6bf3-50f8-af96-25002a1726eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1966 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "319126a6-cb98-5c29-8c30-bfd20133ec43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452443} {:answer/id #uuid "285ae59e-5ddd-5dc9-862d-3bc834ee97db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186