kbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1b3df2e-4878-5f07-9682-4c09eb922bca" :question/math-academy-id "q-97945" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(A+I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- **II.** $\\text{rank}(A+I)=3$\n- **III.** $\\text{Null}(A+I)={0}$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A+I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A+I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A+I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since $M=A+I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A+I)\\ne 3,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A+I)\\ne {0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050470 :topic/id #uuid "4c435cda-6bf3-50f8-af96-25002a1726eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1966 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"} :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487555} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488313}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124605 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Usi