8} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9364d49b-0c70-5f53-9afc-58a6465fdfe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452483} {:answer/id #uuid "bb43d0d6-435a-59ca-b0d6-dd54c6d2f55e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9364d49b-0c70-5f53-9afc-58a6465fdfe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452483} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d70d8de-2041-5147-b2df-9b23496268ca" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fb578155-2783-5751-8e62-1deb89778f31" :question/math-academy-id "q-97970" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(B-I)=1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{Col}(B-I)={R}^{3}$\n- **II.** $B-I$ is singular\n- **III.** $\\lambda =1$ is not an eigenvalue of $B$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(B-I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(B-I) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\text{rank}(B-I)\\ne 3,$ according to invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{Col}(B-I)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(B-I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $B-I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=B-I$ is singular, then $(B-I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda =1$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124529 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050470 :topic/id #uuid "4c435cda-6bf3-50f8-af96-25002a1726eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1966 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2408efc0-effa-5d1a-95bf-4e5888eb4e35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452485} {:answer/id #uuid "478fc76b-1ffb-5eba-9444-fb9a5daab721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2408efc0-effa-5d1a-95bf-4e5888eb4e35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452485} :answer-field/id #uuid "298cd9f6-0f4b-5d91-b1e2-380da3c049cf" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453090} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "890097e8-1b87-5c8b-80f8-73adc10117b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452487} {:answer/id #uuid "194e8931-2