b/ref 17592186468923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cedf372f-ea7d-5fc6-8e97-1703247cb294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468923} :answer-field/id #uuid "72714db6-c639-5979-9a48-742baf128ee3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5c98c0e9-7419-5d7e-b919-df2475fb8298" :question/math-academy-id "q-131025" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-2x}$ is a solution to the equation $(1+2x){y}^{″}+4x{y}^{′}-4y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n- **I.** $y(x)={e}^{4-2x}$\n- **II.** $y(x)=x{e}^{-2x}$\n- **III.** $y(x)=\\frac{2}{{e}^{2x}}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{4-2x}={e}^{4}\\cdot {e}^{-2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c={e}^{4}.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=x{e}^{-2x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{2}{{e}^{2x}}=2\\cdot {e}^{-2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367345 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050032 :topic/id #uuid "277bf882-73ab-515e-84e5-4f7920acb235" :topic/math-academy-id 2549 :topic/title "The Superposition Principle"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016b1440-004a-5e39-8d68-b109db73255c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468924} {:answer/id #uuid "628f0776-ec70-5711-b2f5-0c5156869c39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "628f0776-ec70-5711-b2f5-0c5156869c39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468925} :answer-field/id #uuid "35695d2a-47ab-5e02-95e6-7e73b87bee03" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470074} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a58cccc-4f85-589b-9846-47b6f32f47b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468926} {:answer/id #uuid "aace0540-eb13-5045-a32c-23e9f4d5cd50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a58cccc-4f85-589b-9846-47b6f32f47b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468926} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a0bffd9-93be-5968-a9e9-db0930d4f4d4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470075} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80f02ff4-30f9-57c7-81a9-0b6859391ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468928} {:answer/id #uuid "ccea8c3f-2ba0-5435-ae58-7f06be3c8f08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccea8c3f-2ba0-5435-ae58-7f06be3c8f08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468929} :answer-field/id #uuid "699db024-2862-5b7b-9d8e-321a8c84be8c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e6f1fe4c-12f4-51c1-8c80-ca5bb6fcfc47" :question/math-academy-id "q-131027" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x/3}$ is a solution to the equation $6{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n- **I.** $y(x)={e}^{(x-3)/3}$\n- **II.** $y(x)=\\frac{x}{3}{e}^{x/3}$\n- **III.** $y(x)=\\frac{5{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x/3}}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{(x-3)/3}={e}^{-1}\\cdot {e}^{x/3}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c={e}^{-1}.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{x}{3}{e}^{x/3}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{5{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x/3}}=5\\cdot {e}^{x-2x/3}=5{e}^{x/3}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367352 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Linear ODEs Using the Superposition Principle" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050032 :topic/id #uuid "277bf882-73ab-515e-84e5-4f7920acb235" :topic/math-academy-id 2549 :topic/title "The Superposition Principle"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a110499-d964-53fd-b1cc-a6e49131c202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468930} {:answer/id #uuid "59842690-4580-59b5-a6ed-d947ae64b3c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59842690-4580-59b5-a6ed-d947ae64b3c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468931} :answer-field/id #uuid "72865be2-5ec6-5323-ae78-ecc14e35f121" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470077} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d122ddec-72db-59a3-9fe1-bc707e84fc32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468932} {:answer/id #uuid "bba2240b-c719-5668-8120-54329467c641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bba2240b-c719-5668-8120-54329467c641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468933} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b3790d9-1a04-59a1-bfff-e050665e8224" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470078} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "983ad9d1-7773-5bfc-ba86-dd47a61a4b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468934} {:answer/id #uuid "a28122a7-e37c-5832-948b-fc6df880d5ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a28122a7-e37c-5832-948b-fc6df880d5ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468935} :answer-field/id #uuid "96f2fbcf-6a92-57fa-b4e5-a9504d0e83ee" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5af618e0-efed-5d52-a5fe-f92221b7400c" :question/math-academy-id "q-131038" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{-3t}$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={e}^{-2t}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n- **I.** $y(t)=2{e}^{-3t}+3{e}^{-2t}$\n- **II.** $y(t)=(4+{e}^{t}){e}^{-3t}$\n- **III.** $y(t)=5{e}^{-3t}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(t)$ and ${y}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(t)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(t)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{-3t}$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={e}^{-2t}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}t{e}^{-2t}\n$$\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(t)=2{e}^{-3t}+3{e}^{-2t}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=2,{c}_{2}=3.$\n\n- The function $y(t)=(4+{e}^{t}){e}^{-3t}=4{e}^{-3t}+{e}^{-2t}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=4,{c}_{2}=1.$\n\n- The function $y(t)=5{e}^{-3t}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=5,{c}_{2}=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367387 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115334 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf19e2ee-7c42-58b0-9254-5a44f315288a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Block Diagonal and Block Triangular Matrices" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048221 :topic/id #uuid "8959dd0b-8ef2-54bf-b8d9-d9b2606263cd" :topic/math-academy-id 1735 :topic/title "Partitioned and Block Matrices"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7308f1e2-e007-5c2e-907c-22bc98d0f77f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468936} {:answer/id #uuid "0a28f853-0fa0-505b-b5dc-dabd085a65de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a28f853-0fa0-505b-b5dc-dabd085a65de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468937} :answer-field/id #uuid "e40a305a-51fa-577b-b0d1-2f30645cf2f2" :answer-field/key "A" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470080} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf2571f9-f77c-5433-917a-edf1ee3295de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468938} {:answer/id #uuid "7347e1c9-bf88-5d63-9f71-a549b59a6be4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf2571f9-f77c-5433-917a-edf1ee3295de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468938} :answer-field/id #uuid "36b3f1bf-b723-5f6b-b4f8-d6df672c4826" :answer-field/key "B" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470081} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a528447-3459-57b5-88c5-a12c03f95ffe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468940} {:answer/id #uuid "23b0245a-f580-5cdc-a3a0-64c481c6bc0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23b0245a-f580-5cdc-a3a0-64c481c6bc0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468941} :answer-field/id #uuid "af9bae30-f4e2-59ef-9906-b389016cca44" :answer-field/key "C" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470082}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ba25631a-3eab-56f2-a3d7-d0c6da834d90" :question/math-academy-id "q-131447" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are block diagonal?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A block diagonal matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and all the other blocks contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is a block diagonal matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $B$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $C$ is a block diagonal matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367881 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124570 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049676 :topic/id #uuid "0a177390-6525-5d0c-8721-f7f75db39408" :topic/math-academy-id 1945 :topic/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "caadcd75-2894-55c4-9844-09b832086177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468954} {:answer/id #uuid "95a7a84c-bce3-5f15-881a-dbcea3b99751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95a7a84c-bce3-5f15-881a-dbcea3b99751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468955} :answer-field/id #uuid "a099ca7a-3e8c-5904-be12-6c4b93c0d187" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470089} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aae58fe6-7d2f-510e-8f4d-d82de11d3786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468956} {:answer/id #uuid "ae687bee-0042-50ef-b035-f59c08ad8cb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae687bee-0042-50ef-b035-f59c08ad8cb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468957} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ec929f9-4d85-5417-b2ca-3a0683ff1a3e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470090} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39234e9c-2da3-5747-a131-1a128a6b9bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468958} {:answer/id #uuid "bf82c8a7-ab9f-5ede-99cf-894371a25d1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf82c8a7-ab9f-5ede-99cf-894371a25d1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468959} :answer-field/id #uuid "c14db63c-f2a2-5555-a425-0a6835e03736" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "883c276b-11e1-503d-959e-3a4c3a5e61c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-163777" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈3+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=-2$ and $det(A)=-7$\n- **II.** $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n- **III.** $a$ is a saddle point of $f$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-2$ and\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-7.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-2<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-7<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049676 :topic/id #uuid "0a177390-6525-5d0c-8721-f7f75db39408" :topic/math-academy-id 1945 :topic/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions"} :activity/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556413} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557359}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367888 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124570 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049676 :topic/id #uuid "0a177390-6525-5d0c-8721-f7f75db39408" :topic/math-academy-id 1945 :topic/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79848729-40e3-58f8-aead-25a4bf9f91ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468960} {:answer/id #uuid "bdf43adb-e00a-52d6-9171-6bb57f6c2393" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79848729-40e3-58f8-aead-25a4bf9f91ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468960} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a2e7e84-4d50-5f18-bf38-f3d4119b4f61" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470092} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1aeab25-5623-5fb1-a33c-8659586a7265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468962} {:answer/id #uuid "000a5f85-f437-53f8-913c-2f859328fe33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1aeab25-5623-5fb1-a33c-8659586a7265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468962} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ea7aae1-f019-57b9-9bd5-f09302e71e14" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470093} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e948e36-b6c1-5434-958f-e2bbe1ea693a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468964} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6e86d7-eddf-593d-8716-1c788f8c5e9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f6e86d7-eddf-593d-8716-1c788f8c5e9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468965} :answer-field/id #uuid "431cae4d-364e-588f-aeb2-fee9cea68018" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "61ffb698-9cc2-5e02-b6d9-afec7d1f3422" :question/math-academy-id "q-163790" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-7+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $det(A)>0$\n- **II.** $A$ is positive-definite\n- **III.** $a$ is a local maximum of $f$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -4\\end{aligned}|+4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-5(11)-2(-4)+4(10) \\\\  & =-7<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-5<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}|=11>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-7<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049676 :topic/id #uuid "0a177390-6525-5d0c-8721-f7f75db39408" :topic/math-academy-id 1