4}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1b0f0f8-1d9c-57ac-b3d1-98b74059f05b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451824} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd47b439-ba64-5e6b-bc45-8dfabdcbeee8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452759} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7928aa54-54af-5cdc-9bbb-4e065da5a513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451825} {:answer/id #uuid "59339a2c-ff4f-58f7-b8e7-0d95783a0f53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59339a2c-ff4f-58f7-b8e7-0d95783a0f53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451826} :answer-field/id #uuid "c200a0c7-db5f-5d96-89b1-1917c24bb9ca" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7ba712ba-8939-58aa-ab47-15caf19c7eaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-81165" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-\\frac{y}{x} & \\frac{x}{y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(e,e).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $H(e,e)>0$\n- **II.** $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(e,e)>0$\n- **III.** The point $(e,e)$ is a saddle point of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-\\frac{y}{x} & \\frac{x}{y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-\\frac{y}{x})=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{y}-\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{y}-\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}-\\frac{1}{x} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(e,e),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(e,e)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{e} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{e}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(e,e),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(e,e)=det({f}^{″}(e,e))=\\frac{1}{e}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{e})-{0}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(e,e)=({f}^{″}(e,e))_{11}=\\frac{1}{e}>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(e,e)<0,$ the point $(e,e)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368791 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Critical Point Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051048 :topic/id #uuid "7d157e41-c50b-570b-8b5b-b8a5a0d03163" :topic/math-academy-id 1944 :topic/title "The Second Partial Derivatives Test"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2db97ae9-3132-5f60-9505-52d59d6093b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451827} {:answer/id #uuid "cd78b809-fc70-5409-bdfe-c595704b483e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2db97ae9-3132-5f60-9505-52d59d6093b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451827} :answer-field/id #uuid "e29ae991-f973-5af1-93a8-4cd84016ebb2" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452761} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "466b4dbb-70e4-56ca-9322-162cbda3357c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451829} {:answer/id #uuid "c0ffb5aa-785e-58f2-b3fe-d898c76316e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "466b4dbb-70e4-56ca-9322-162cbda3357c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451829} :answer-field/id #uuid "08547b44-109f-53c8-b356-c736674a80cc" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452762} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b51eaf02-186e-526f-a02a-9cc23128ac67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451831} {:answer/id #uuid "30a287b8-b29e-5198-bf8f-109b83e4de33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30a287b8-b29e-5198-bf8f-109b83e4de33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451832} :answer-field/id #uuid "0879547d-46f5-5728-a5f8-f9d6a9520f45" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ce3eb01-5fdc-5cad-ba01-77459c09b714" :question/math-academy-id "q-81166" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2xy & 2xy+{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(0,0).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $H(0,0)>0$\n- **II.** $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)<0$\n- **III.** The second partial derivative test at point $(0,0)$ is inconclusive" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2xy & 2xy+{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2xy,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2xy+{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2xy)=6x+2y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2})=2x+6y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2})=2y+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6x+2y & 2x+2y \\\\ 2x+2y & 2x+6y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=0\\cdot 0-{0}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,0)=({f}^{″}(0,0))_{11}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $H(0,0)=0,$ the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184197 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8063bc9c-a10d-562b-93b1-508b4b26a674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Singular Values of a Matrix" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049462 :topic/id #uuid "f7488799-2322-5ff9-8065-6852954f5eb5" :topic/math-academy-id 3819 :topic/title "Computing the Singular Values of a Matrix"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dd7d102-aedc-5e91-a047-f8cad7922fa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452179} {:answer/id #uuid "92081811-29e7-5671-8299-e57c420e3eed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2dd7d102-aedc-5e91-a047-f8cad7922fa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452179} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8ec87bb-4b3c-5297-b2b5-774e119551e7" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452937} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f401cf9-efdd-54a5-8b7a-61de53c5e100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452181} {:answer/id #uuid "d311e63e-ec0f-59e7-bd16-ff6db836531a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f401cf9-efdd-54a5-8b7a-61de53c5e100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452181} :answer-field/id #uuid "968b4eb6-8e02-5cf8-a607-4efe45260c79" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452938} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "097bc3b9-dca8-57a1-8757-cc94a3779994" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452183} {:answer/id #uuid "862c2197-4e1a-56eb-9ab6-61a342ae1dbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "862c2197-4e1a-56eb-9ab6-61a342ae1dbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452184} :answer-field/id #uuid "60e094a4-a998-50ed-b26d-1c216b5e4e7c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e856448-81c6-5a27-b63c-c2864d6eca93" :question/math-academy-id "q-95291" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Any matrix of the form  $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-a & 0 \\\\ 0 & a\\end{bmatrix}$ has one negative singular value.\n- **II.** Any $5\\times 2$ matrix $A$ has at most $5$ distinct singular values.\n- **III.** Any $4\\times 5$ matrix $A$ has at most $5$ distinct singular values." :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false.  All singular values of any matrix are non-negative. So, \n$A$  can't have any negative singular value.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $A$ is a $5\\times 2$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $2\\times 5$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $2\\times 2$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{2\\times 5}\\times {A}_{5\\times 2}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $2$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement III is true.  Indeed,  since $A$ is a $4\\times 5$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $5\\times 4$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $5\\times 5$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{5\\times 4}\\times {A}_{4\\times 5}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{5\\times 5}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $5$ distinct singular values.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368948 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049741 :topic/id #uuid "10441679-94f4-5ae1-8bff-8e5945144d9c" :topic/math-academy-id 2532 :topic/title "Phase Lines of First-Order ODEs"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b00deac4-82bd-58a5-bb08-8958694c8af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469910} {:answer/id #uuid "21967399-dccf-54ec-b7cd-77db99d92fec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21967399-dccf-54ec-b7cd-77db99d92fec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469911} :answer-field/id #uuid "d846a42d-87d4-54e7-8f12-7aa06935045d" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470567} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c91c9077-7e88-5448-961a-13f8598b152d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469912} {:answer/id #uuid "7bab4da9-6ee0-5390-bb08-209ac71b5d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c91c9077-7e88-5448-961a-13f8598b152d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469912} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec0432b6-51c6-5aef-b5ee-73f852d7ec46" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470568} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "642ae02b-a718-55a7-8a2b-a04233ed13c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469914} {:answer/id #uuid "d9c3a0bc-093c-55ab-8ac9-46a1335901ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "642ae02b-a718-55a7-8a2b-a04233ed13c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469914} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba45bfce-366e-5800-999f-3a5fb03af237" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5a7147e0-2667-5230-a581-5d8792b92824" :question/math-academy-id "q-98642" :question/problem "![](images/cf/cff9a8967582489286a62b78234e147684d1e453c20967fe1190cf515afe246f.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}(y+3)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y(y+3)^{2}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+3y$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-3$ and $y=0.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-3,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-3).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(y+3).$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)={y}^{2}(y+3),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-3,0.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,0)$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+3$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| ${y}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for  $y\\in (-\\infty ,-3),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-3,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation I has the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=y(y+3)^{2}.$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=y(y+3)^{2},$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-3,0.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)<0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (-3,0).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}+3y.$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=y(y+3),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-3,0.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)<0$ for $y\\in (-3,0).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation III does not have the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368955 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049741 :topic/id #uuid "10441679-94f4-5ae1-8bff-8e5945144d9c" :topic/math-academy-id 2532 :topic/title "Phase Lines of First-Order ODEs"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c903d023-82cb-575f-bbad-9bd9310edeaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469928} {:answer/id #uuid "43a38e11-a94b-5269-a76d-bacbb18c259e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c903d023-82cb-575f-bbad-9bd9310edeaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469928} :answer-field/id #uuid "8aecb6bb-e015-5a8f-9684-dbd58074074b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470576} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ab78c2c-2cbb-5521-9a73-957b25aefe5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469930} {:answer/id #uuid "db9452fa-5d55-5d15-9dd9-adec263c54a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db9452fa-5d55-5d15-9dd9-adec263c54a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469931} :answer-field/id #uuid "c37d880d-82f7-54f7-8885-1a6d3faaa263" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470577} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6a7981e-9f86-5208-a719-0a5a7abc361e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469932} {:answer/id #uuid "17433fa2-c947-5eb2-80ad-94be5e6ef43a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6a7981e-9f86-5208-a719-0a5a7abc361e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469932} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cf764df-de26-514e-9f0c-5e2750357d6b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bfb99b4c-3f9c-571a-8cfe-9d0080c591ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-98647" :question/problem "![](images/a7/a7f21df22875efb2457fcf82e616778c9f61f77843b6969c964a293f9e113a5a.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)(y-4)^{2}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-6y+8$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{2}+6y-8$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=2$ and $y=4.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (4,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (2,4).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=(y-2)(y-4)^{2}.$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=(y-2)(y-4)^{2},$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=2,4.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (2,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-6y+8.$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=(y-2)(y-4),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=2,4.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $(2,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for  $y\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (4,\\infty ),$ and $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (2,4).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II has the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}=-{y}^{2}+6y-8.$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=-(y-2)(y-4),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=2,4.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (2,4).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation III does not have the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083201 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048115 :topic/id #uuid "80368ff1-7296-5f6c-aa32-63547e29e578" :topic/math-academy-id 758 :topic/title "Improper Integrals"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc0f0bbf-9e98-54b3-bf3e-86d3585281f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468852} {:answer/id #uuid "0364d1e1-fd92-5dce-b6af-859298fcbb61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0364d1e1-fd92-5dce-b6af-859298fcbb61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468853} :answer-field/id #uuid "56a22fa4-849c-5c90-9822-62f2fcf23aaf" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470038} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "308906a6-4c6a-54ce-a9d9-4762d925306b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468854} {:answer/id #uuid "d4f5d776-8ae6-5de7-96df-59aa6fc9e843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4f5d776-8ae6-5de7-96df-59aa6fc9e843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468855} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cdd4872-c6a0-59bf-8e3c-aadb70e9ae28" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470039} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1935d638-cd1f-5a78-8128-5b81e02f9c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468856} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5e324c-feff-5279-9a68-2ee89b03c35d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1935d638-cd1f-5a78-8128-5b81e02f9c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468856} :answer-field/id #uuid "b36b9451-aa6f-5293-a01d-78f77c5a1f3f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ebe5f91b-e612-5062-b7df-947a793c33b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-101734" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-5}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-5}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{6}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[2\\sqrt{x-5}]}_{6}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-5}-2\\sqrt{6-5}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-5}-2) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}\\,{x}^{-2/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{x}^{1/3}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\sqrt[3]{a}-1) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1+\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only integrals I and II are divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179743 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1da220a9-2afc-5b63-a423-a4d2b3de9567" :knowledge-point/title "Circulation in Conservative Vector Fields" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050773 :topic/id #uuid "659072cc-8b2c-5e8d-b6a6-88321581f47f" :topic/math-academy-id 3713 :topic/title "Circulation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42ccf9fe-9fff-57a9-9