17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471400} :answer-field/id #uuid "faf4f041-9d66-5c32-91ff-4106fe85d773" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472205} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1f2baf0-4b87-5c2b-9ea5-eeef4731f725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471401} {:answer/id #uuid "09474881-68d0-5b18-95f0-b3882e547c0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1f2baf0-4b87-5c2b-9ea5-eeef4731f725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471401} :answer-field/id #uuid "06f4b1d1-82b6-5b27-958b-7d47672530ae" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7357f0db-ce2b-5133-a2b6-cf34bdf9d888" :question/math-academy-id "q-334012" :question/problem "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method **unstable**, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n- **I.** $Δx=\\frac{2.5}{\\lambda }$\n- **II.** $Δx=\\frac{0.6}{\\lambda }$\n- **III.** $Δx=\\frac{3}{\\lambda }$\n- **IV.** $Δx=\\frac{1.9}{\\lambda }$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function for Euler's method is given by $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$\n\nNow, we consider each step size in turn.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{2.5}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=2.5\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls outside the given region, Euler's method is not stable (or unstable) for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{0.6}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=0.6.$ Since this value falls within the given region,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & <\\lambda Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <0.6<2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method must be stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{3}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=3\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls outside the given region, Euler's method is not stable (or unstable) for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{1.9}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=1.9.$ Since this value falls within the given region,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & <\\lambda Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <1.9<2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method must be stable for this step size.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372670 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229159 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4823cf5-2656-5616-b4af-193f70c3f2bc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Euler's Method" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049575 :topic/id #uuid "01533816-c819-5cab-8910-7cf6afa63383" :topic/math-academy-id 6685 :topic/title "Stability of Numerical Methods"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "366d8acf-79b7-5e71-9b0d-049e02cb0fcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471403} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce1e8fa-d6aa-5d53-8c65-6c893ade9430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "366d8acf-79b7-5e71-9b0d-049e02cb0fcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471403} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e3e43d5-60c1-55d5-a665-f4184d96dd42" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472207} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55e12b5c-4eaa-50c7-8d9e-a394ac5ce0f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471405} {:answer/id #uuid "adeaf175-81b3-562a-88d9-68b0a9c47518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55e12b5c-4eaa-50c7-8d9e-a394ac5ce0f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471405} :answer-field/id #uuid "05623d44-5548-54f0-8992-9102964353ae" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472208} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bcd624de-ae51-5456-9cb0-dfe46fc51e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471407} {:answer/id #uuid "e0d91a6e-4513-5074-8afc-edf6bb57be63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0d91a6e-4513-5074-8afc-edf6bb57be63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471408} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1540473-6fc2-51de-b17e-00b3c98bfe13" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472209} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e130b4a-3867-5084-8cfe-ace4fdd806cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471409} {:answer/id #uuid "1d184079-8503-55d5-bb28-d8b23c4a8384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d184079-8503-55d5-bb28-d8b23c4a8384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471410} :answer-field/id #uuid "b55194fe-6c23-5236-bc2a-f288f1a6a87a" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9c74e09c-c853-5496-bb2c-3a8ba557b3ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-334178" :question/problem "For which of the following values is Euler's method **unstable**, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.5$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=1.8$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=2.1$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=3$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$ Of the given values:\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.5,$ falls *within* the given region,\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.8,$ falls *within* the given region,\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.1,$ falls *outside* the given region.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=3,$ falls *outside* the given region.\n\nHence, Euler's method is **unstable** for the values $\\lambda Δx=2.1,$ and $\\lambda Δx=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III and IV only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "151add00-204f-5795-aff9-cceddc26b24b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Other Methods" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049575 :topic/id #uuid "01533816-c819-5cab-8910-7cf6afa63383" :topic/math-academy-id 6685 :topic/title "Stability of Numerical Methods"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a236ee0f-4a3d-55a5-9a29-b092842930da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471419} {:answer/id #uuid "4450a753-5992-5151-b771-796d2894c70a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4450a753-5992-5151-b771-796d2894c70a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471420} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7700a96-7ad6-5083-ad7c-8e281c670000" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472215} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9763120-6606-599b-934b-95db763e1233" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471421} {:answer/id #uuid "4b4cc027-2dc9-5355-9d88-5ae327c54cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b4cc027-2dc9-5355-9d88-5ae327c54cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471422} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e42b658-9bec-5e39-bc22-0ea6b0feedc4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472216} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f648fda4-e87f-566a-a566-4e09bc924214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471423} {:answer/id #uuid "4ea37cf6-6012-5d5e-84a5-417006c11a53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f648fda4-e87f-566a-a566-4e09bc924214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471423} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8e99c3d-6364-5eaf-82d2-dbbe369ebad8" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472217} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a41646e7-d2e0-53ec-8d1a-a1a417903331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471425} {:answer/id #uuid "01fd0090-091c-5732-9466-b423e9e87912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a41646e7-d2e0-53ec-8d1a-a1a417903331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471425} :answer-field/id #uuid "1852daa2-566c-5147-b4a4-9116977c2ae4" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd6bd92c-8765-5d3c-9d52-912010fd3181" :question/math-academy-id "q-334425" :question/problem "For which of the following values is the Runge-Kutta Method (RK4) stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n- **I.** $\\lambda Δx=0.3$\n- **II.** $\\lambda Δx=2.4$\n- **III.** $\\lambda Δx=2.8$\n- **IV.** $\\lambda Δx=3$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the RK4 method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method  ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the RK4 Method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the RK4 Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.3)| & =|1-0.3+\\frac{(0.3)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0.3)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(0.3)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{59\\,267}{80\\,000}| \\\\  & =0.740…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.4)| & =|1-2.4+\\frac{(2.4)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(2.4)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(2.4)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{349}{625}| \\\\  & =0.558…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.8)| & =|1-2.8+\\frac{(2.8)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(2.8)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(2.8)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{639}{625}| \\\\  & =1.0224>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3)| & =|1-3+\\frac{(3)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(3)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(3)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{11}{8}| \\\\  & =1.375<1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the RK4 Method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2.785…,$ so it is not absolutely stable."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372684 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "151add00-204f-5795-aff9-cceddc26b24b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Other Methods" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049575 :topic/id #uuid "01533816-c819-5cab-8910-7cf6afa63383" :topic/math-academy-id 6685 :topic/title "Stability of Numerical Methods"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcfddc24-c58f-5c4f-84e9-ef551982185b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471427} {:answer/id #uuid "9769274f-fb36-5fbc-8541-fc90883166a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9769274f-fb36-5fbc-8541-fc90883166a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471428} :answer-field/id #uuid "41a7fbf7-0d41-5041-aae1-a39e147b182f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472219} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f185f3b-0a68-5abd-a8c2-38bf014f58b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471429} {:answer/id #uuid "fa8a98cb-e1a9-56a3-92c7-055782d9153d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa8a98cb-e1a9-56a3-92c7-055782d9153d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471430} :answer-field/id #uuid "74f644d8-2edd-5b0b-937d-f2bfddc4567b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472220} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "088c5cde-2edc-582f-8a1e-928e3b8226d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471431} {:answer/id #uuid "dfda1d96-e20f-55b1-8aa9-981ec2a0f203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "088c5cde-2edc-582f-8a1e-928e3b8226d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471431} :answer-field/id #uuid "79254c88-eae6-5edf-bb54-1cc11619fc3f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472221} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "753fff6f-e13f-5c5d-ae71-087ae88e049e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471433} {:answer/id #uuid "e59e1c38-8632-55cd-89ae-49999007c409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e59e1c38-8632-55cd-89ae-49999007c409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471434} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b3380c0-50d4-5961-9f40-7aaca9597d4f" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ea2a49c-9462-54d3-b8e9-50d1002b7880" :question/math-academy-id "q-334775" :question/problem "For which of the following values is the modified Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n- **I.** $\\lambda Δx=0.65$\n- **II.** $\\lambda Δx=1.35$\n- **III.** $\\lambda Δx=3.6$\n- **IV.** $\\lambda Δx=1.25$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the modified Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the modified Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the modified Euler method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.65,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.65)| & =|1-0.65+\\frac{(0.65)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{449}{800}| \\\\  & =0.561\\,25<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.35,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.35)| & =|1-1.35+\\frac{(1.35)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{449}{800}| \\\\  & =0.561\\,25<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=3.6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3.6)| & =|1-3.6+\\frac{(3.6)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{97}{25}| \\\\  & =3.88>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the modified Euler method is unstable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.25,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.25)| & =|1-1.25+\\frac{(1.25)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{17}{32}| \\\\  & =0.531\\,25<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Since this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the modified Euler method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2,$ so it is not absolutely stable." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049575 :topic/id #uuid "01533816-c819-5cab-8910-7cf6afa63383" :topic/math-academy-id 6685 :topic/title "Stability of Numerical Methods"} :activity/title "Stability of Numerical Methods" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518989} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519669}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373132 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124475 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74f126da-5f3b-5af8-9aee-d6f0a4c61c49" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Shape of a Level Curve" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049882 :topic/id #uuid "1ad374f3-e4f4-5dcc-ab6d-d6aed0790f3a" :topic/math-academy-id 1900 :topic/title "Level Curves"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de1b2908-d140-5d0a-8262-c958d76bfa4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471471} {:answer/id #uuid "f456143a-b430-5e18-95eb-88219103f545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de1b2908-d140-5d0a-8262-c958d76bfa4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471471} :answer-field/id #uuid "06e697f7-446e-5fcc-ae8a-db378503de70" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472241} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55e41eb5-2115-5b1f-9bbb-e67f510d8e1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471473} {:answer/id #uuid "bf770033-4a57-5465-a9ee-d6d68d54b733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf770033-4a57-5465-a9ee-d6d68d54b733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471474} :answer-field/id #uuid "83576e31-5a8a-5315-8054-127553d4c6a1" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472242} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad5d90de-a61d-5e9f-a889-d499427c60be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471475} {:answer/id #uuid "9f5c3a1e-f107-5159-ae8f-6ce7a0d2da41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad5d90de-a61d-5e9f-a889-d499427c60be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471475} :answer-field/id #uuid "5771a531-83c1-5b90-a459-0ec86b28bfd9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c853fbcf-5be0-5101-8f2a-558cbd4a782f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71402" :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=9x+3y-4$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $c=0$ gives an ellipse\n- **II.** $c=-4$ gives a line\n- **III.** $c=5$ gives a hyperbola" :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9x+3y-4 & =c \\\\ 3y & =-9x+c+4 \\\\ y & =-3x+\\frac{c+4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=0$ then the level curve is $y=-3x+\\frac{4}{3},$ which is not an ellipse.\n\n- If $c=-4$ then the level curve is $y=-3x,$ which is a line.\n\n- If $c=5$ then the level curve is $y=-3x+3,$ which is not a hyperbola.\n\nSo, only statement II is true." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/title "Quiz 16" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045460 :db/ident :activity.type/assessment} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502975} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503836}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124485 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "486cc512-416b-5eac-8964-1a87d533de72" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Given Set Is Linearly Independent Given Information on Its Elements" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048257 :topic/id #uuid "8cc35c33-61af-5ac3-971b-a33b8bbdcb9d" :topic/math-academy-id 1908 :topic/title "Linear Independence in Abstract Vector Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6429683f-31e8-587f-9bb6-943f9c82808e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453204} {:answer/id #uuid "4cdec651-22d0-5cdc-8ed3-7bdaf0df5030" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6429683f-31e8-587f-9bb6-943f9c82808e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453204} :answer-field/id #uuid "9880c020-21e3-5d95-a6ac-b7dd403e79c4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454352} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d39f87e-17b4-54ca-8e3d-abd7a0fd1e75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453206} {:answer/id #uuid "cf8f6a1e-a71e-5ccf-b95b-576393c72cb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf8f6a1e-a71e-5ccf-b95b-576393c72cb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453207} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d0c4844-ac6c-5d61-8dbe-13c93d0977ea" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454353} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03783e7e-3525-506f-8dfe-fc9257d75eff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453208} {:answer/id #uuid "f408e24a-eb89-5177-aca3-74153b203cb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03783e7e-3525-506f-8dfe-fc9257d75eff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453208} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3d35fc4-4a66-5588-9bb4-1ae54e82809d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3e3aa161-9904-5811-8bd5-9983d559a3c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-107047" :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\in V.$ Given that $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\{{v}_{1}-{v}_{2},{v}_{2}-{v}_{3},{v}_{1}-{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent\n\n- **II.** $\\{{v}_{1},2{v}_{2},3{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent\n\n- **III.** $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},0\\}$ is linearly independent" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot ({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})+1\\cdot ({v}_{2}-{v}_{3})+(-1)\\cdot ({v}_{1}-{v}_{3})=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})+{x}_{2}({v}_{2}-{v}_{3})+{x}_{3}({v}_{1}-{v}_{3})=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that $\\{{v}_{1}-{v}_{2},{v}_{2}-{v}_{3},{v}_{1}-{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}(2{v}_{2})+{x}_{3}(3{v}_{3}) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{v}_{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of the system is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ which means that \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},2{v}_{2},3{v}_{3}\\}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, notice that \n\n$$\n0\\cdot {v}_{1}+0\\cdot {v}_{2}+1\\cdot 0=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}0=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},0\\}$ is linearly dependent. In general, any set containing the zero vector is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048257 :topic/id #uuid "8cc35c33-61af-5ac3-971b-a33b8bbdcb9d" :topic/math-academy-id 1908 :topic/title "Linear Independence in Abstract Vector Spaces"} :activity/title "Linear Independence in Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436336} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436347}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373757 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124456 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84245283-3cf9-5889-8c42-896a0c8af291" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points on a Curve at which the Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to a Given Vector" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049511 :topic/id #uuid "fabe76f4-9f30-5b59-b906-f5fa71cfb89f" :topic/math-academy-id 1940 :topic/title "Tangent Lines to Level Curves"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afbb76f8-a135-56ab-98ff-95aa371de6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439519} {:answer/id #uuid "8c88915e-5b21-5b50-b76c-b452eb74095d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afbb76f8-a135-56ab-98ff-95aa371de6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439519} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb7991eb-11ab-5be8-9cff-8cb4c831a986" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440117} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bee1c3fd-7f28-5bd0-8439-264f31d9f15a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439521} {:answer/id #uuid "aba6457d-6ee5-5643-8c8f-eae80358d7d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aba6457d-6ee5-5643-8c8f-eae80358d7d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439522} :answer-field/id #uuid "f05aaa51-707b-51f8-bed1-c062eb9077c6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440118} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "049822ce-ef15-5b5d-9864-ade62a37ec43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439523} {:answer/id #uuid "60358451-984b-5b61-929f-f92074afc976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60358451-984b-5b61-929f-f92074afc976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439524} :answer-field/id #uuid "24967753-af42-5895-a050-8e8c6b225ad6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440119} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a274467-574a-5539-983e-4d583bc9ec92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439525} {:answer/id #uuid "65a64ec9-6cdd-507e-9bfb-590aab6a8b2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a274467-574a-5539-983e-4d583bc9ec92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439525} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed98d8f5-235d-543c-a23d-ff763ed379a9" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3d9c5851-2645-50db-8b4e-b731824fd7d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-130225" :question/problem "At which points on the curve $2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}=-y$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j?$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $(-1,1)$\n- **II.** $(0,0)$\n- **III.** $(0,\\frac{1}{3})$\n- **IV.** $(1,1)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2} & =-y \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+y)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+y)\\,j \\\\  & =(-6y+1)\\,i-4x\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j,$ we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ ((-6y+1)\\,i-4x\\,j)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ -4x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2} & =-y \\\\ 2({0}^{2})-3{y}^{2} & =-y \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-y & =0 \\\\ y(3y-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding points are $(0,0)$ and $(0,\\frac{1}{3}).$" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049511 :topic/id #uuid "fabe76f4-9f30-5b59-b906-f5fa71cfb89f" :topic/math-academy-id 1940 :topic/title "Tangent Lines to Level Curves"} :activity/title "Tangent Lines to Level Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481766} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482651}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152101 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa42f9bc-e3ad-56fa-8d82-4882e95ef826" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Disjoint Sets" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048864 :topic/id #uuid "c788c893-5c00-5b28-be4c-bd65dc3ed101" :topic/math-academy-id 2789 :topic/title "Disjoint Sets"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33f674a4-7e0c-5322-95cd-c4b2362d53fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471161