, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The point $A$ is *not* a boundary point because there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained outside the set $M.$\n\n![](images/32/32355d3eedd213884719eafff85f234648c78787a5d6d5940e09bbf2ab19e956.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/11/1137f0953feea39d9f9e90f5e052c07fbbcc1a26527a10e212b2b39f2c6e104e.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](images/f0/f09608f9fca8c564ada0d5239a98c113820e1ec04ff36e8b5300f4161ddaca56.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only.'"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048942 :topic/id #uuid "cdb710e4-10cf-5554-8041-f24c448bbdae" :topic/math-academy-id 1930 :topic/title "Interior and Boundary Points"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8be07d40-14cd-501c-9625-428f6314b23c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471107} {:answer/id #uuid "5e1fbc33-9b75-535