8-fe46-5fc2-980a-0aa8896177fe" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b89dc225-ab3f-5473-8d69-c79632ad45e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-159498" :question/problem "![](images/82/8233b3903cc75e8b2a6d9a341d89fd1524f843c3fb112e6380a8e6275ed50456.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/4b/4b89766f6ee2794b18a7fa5dc549de62df410942cf68cac9629dd696e8f0d906.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$ The point $B$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and III only'."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376157 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092339 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd399539-33cf-5738-b970-fcc6c1d919c8" :knowledge-point/title "Using Properties of Indicator Functions" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048808 :topic/id #uuid "c1647abf-57b4-5851-a828-2952c6947148" :topic/math-academy-id 948 :topic/title "Indicator Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4845966-372c-548d-a64a-8d5c7c19d0ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453580} {:answer/id #uuid "f1849a3b-58bc-5f4a-b012-a58f9871612b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4845966-372c-548d-a64a-8d5c7c19d0ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453580} :answer-field/id #uuid "d47284cf-891c-5cb8-ba28-4824c46e48a5" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454540} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e37544ac-2fca-57be-8001-9189226db946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453582} {:answer/id #uuid "70351b57-7f19-5fbe-be03-fa7da2c1b679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70351b57-7f19-5fbe-be03-fa7da2c1b679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453583} :answer-field/id #uuid "69249cbb-7e3c-531b-bd6d-21bc466588de" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454541} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "433a72ab-7d38-5e4a-8f83-e3fe8097b4f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453584} {:answer/id #uuid "09e2b953-fb38-58ce-a5b6-d43d2e3d4354" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "433a72ab-7d38-5e4a-8f83-e3fe8097b4f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453584} :answer-field/id #uuid "e58739c5-1889-5e03-9e74-ee19d0f13dfc" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10dc60fb-f25e-52f7-9f87-59ad72f93d31" :question/math-academy-id "q-174392" :question/problem "$$\n{1}_{A}(c)=0,\\,{1}_{B}(c)=1\n$$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(c)=1$\n- **II.** ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(c)=0$\n- **III.** $c\\in A\\cap B$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(c) & =min{{1}_{A}(c),{1}_{B}(c)} \\\\  & =min{0,1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(c)=0,$ we conclude that $c$ does not belong to $A\\cap B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376707 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203911 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5725601-8181-582a-935d-d3dfde851d63" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functional Relations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048347 :topic/id #uuid "978ab69b-7484-5456-ab65-6064e381c331" :topic/math-academy-id 4825 :topic/title "Functional Relations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7fc041e-c282-53eb-a8fb-17683d9e91b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471371} {:answer/id #uuid "6bbff658-86dd-56c3-85fb-1774d488ff70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7fc041e-c282-53eb-a8fb-17683d9e91b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471371} :answer-field/id #uuid "78749954-9c82-5d36-aec7-fd4ea8273546" :answer-field/key "R" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472191} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63b147bd-19f2-51fc-bc81-f4a381a9136b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471373} {:answer/id #uuid "a5356e06-be98-5dcc-9cf5-65fdd6f7ee01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5356e06-be98-5dcc-9cf5-65fdd6f7ee01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471374} :answer-field/id #uuid "41342172-0d32-5257-82bd-9001c09a991c" :answer-field/key "S" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472192} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d524e9f7-3941-5d66-8a06-46fbe008780d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471375} {:answer/id #uuid "0542e442-4632-53a4-a7fa-0a4416f29cab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d524e9f7-3941-5d66-8a06-46fbe008780d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471375} :answer-field/id #uuid "c209c1d3-98a7-5e7d-b998-6962a694bfb2" :answer-field/key "T" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd2e7ad1-df50-507b-b322-c68735f660cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-216886" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are functional?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **R.** $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x={y}^{2},$ defined on $R$\n\n- **S.** $x\\,S\\,y$ if and only if $x{y}^{3}=1,$ defined on ${R}^{+}$\n\n- **T.** $x\\,T\\,y$ if and only if $y∣2x,$ defined on $Z$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined over $A$ and $B$ is *functional* (or a *partial function*) if, for all $a\\in A$ and ${b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\in B,$ we have \n\n$$\n(a,{b}_{1})\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(a,{b}_{2})\\in R\\,⇒\\,{b}_{1}={b}_{2}.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the first components of two ordered pairs are equal, then the second components must also be equal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- $R$ is *not* functional. Notice that $1={1}^{2}$ and $1=(-1)^{2}.$ So, $R$ contains pairs $(1,1)$ and $(1,-1)$ with the same first component but different second components.\n\n- $S$ is functional. Indeed, $a{b}_{1}^{3}=1$ and $a{b}_{2}^{3}=1$ is equivalent to ${b}_{1}={b}_{2}$ over ${R}^{+}.$ So, when the first components of two pairs are equal, then the second components are also equal.\n\n- $T$ is *not* functional. Notice that $2∣2(3)$ and $3∣2(3).$ So, $T$ contains pairs $(3,2)$ and $(3,3)$ with the same first component but different second components.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$S$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377648 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048942 :topic/id #uuid "cdb710e4-10cf-5554-8041-f24c448bbdae" :topic/math-academy-id 1930 :topic/title "Interior and Boundary Points"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87e9b7a8-66bb-5d09-bd58-7f87a8bd1e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471453} {:answer/id #uuid "e97876f9-dc61-5e39-8076-e12233f6be8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87e9b7a8-66bb-5d09-bd58-7f87a8bd1e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471453} :answer-field/id #uuid "8280f47e-8f45-599c-bff2-457d358b2b6a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472232} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f72681b-5388-5ed3-af01-b1576e6f61e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471455} {:answer/id #uuid "d7d0bd01-3ea0-5efa-8d77-62f3532e9944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7d0bd01-3ea0-5efa-8d77-62f3532e9944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471456} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae5ad6b4-ee02-5fd9-aab5-6a8880027d51" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472233} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99dde3fb-5ec8-5777-87bd-ade4d80c1969" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471457} {:answer/id #uuid "b9a22ad6-93c9-579f-a9f6-63ef19bcd4ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99dde3fb-5ec8-5777-87bd-ade4d80c1969" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471457} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b60aa23-b672-5281-b5ed-2e35c18d69b6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10cee0d6-c023-521a-a779-cab7a62d3b8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-64999" :question/problem "![](images/7f/7ffcda47f0d750ce9f25e50b0b28091ff4279467313fbe604eac0fc9b1229806.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nII. $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nIII. $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/08/080d6cf1cd468b7ddfe65df2e6a21792e1afe470ddd571c6b5ec25569d1fb537.png)\n\n- Statement I is false. Every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to \n$M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$ The point $A$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377655 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowled